**The bug:** The function has no check for empty strings. It only verifies the input isn't `null`, `undefined`, or `false`, then immediately returns `true`. Since `""` is not `null`, not `undefined`, and not `false`, it passes the guard and returns `true`.

**Why it causes the observed behavior:** `validateInput("")` enters the `if` block, passes all three inequality checks, and returns `true` without ever testing whether the string is empty.

**The fix:** Add a check for the empty string before returning `true`.

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function validateInput(input) {
    if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false) {
        if (input === "") {
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

// validateInput("")         -> false
// validateInput(null)       -> false
// validateInput(undefined)  -> false
// validateInput("hello")    -> true
</solution>

