## Diagnosis

The problem is the classic **Go loop variable capture** issue. In Go 1.21, loop variables are **shared** across all iterations — each goroutine does not get its own copy of `i`; they all reference the same variable. By the time any goroutine actually runs and executes `fmt.Println(i)`, the `for` loop has already finished and `i` has been incremented to **5** (the value that fails the `i < 5` check). Every goroutine therefore prints that same final value: `5`.

(Starting with Go 1.22 the loop variable is captured per-iteration, so this bug would disappear on its own — but you're on 1.21, so you need an explicit fix.)

## Fix

The minimal change is to pass a **copy** of `i` into the goroutine as a parameter, so each goroutine captures the value at the moment of spawning:

```go
go func(j int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(j)
}(i)
```

Or equivalently, assign to a local variable before launching the goroutine.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(j int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(j)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

